    question: 'The product is launching across several EU markets. What does that actually require from the frontend beyond translated strings?',
    answer: [
      'Lead with the thing that breaks first, which is string concatenation, because it is the habit that makes everything else impossible. A sentence assembled from fragments encodes English word order and English grammar, and there is no translation of a fragment that works once the target language reorders the clause or inflects the noun. The unit of translation is a whole message with named placeholders, and the moment a message has a number, a gender or a condition in it, that logic belongs inside the message rather than in the component. That is what ICU MessageFormat is for, and saying so is the difference between having done this and having translated a file.',
      'Get plurals right specifically, because English trains people to believe there are two cases. CLDR defines up to six plural categories and languages use different subsets: English has one and other, Polish has one, few, many and other, Russian is similar, Arabic uses all six, and Japanese has only other. So a ternary on count equals one is a bug in most of the EU, and Intl.PluralRules or an ICU plural block is the correct mechanism. Add the related trap: a select for grammatical gender is needed in languages where an adjective or participle agrees with the subject, which has no English equivalent at all and is invisible until a translator raises it.',
      'Put every formatted value through the Intl APIs rather than through anything hand-written, and be specific about what they cover, since this is the part that silently looks wrong rather than breaking: NumberFormat for grouping and decimal separators — a German user reads one thousand as 1.000,00 and a hand-rolled formatter shows them a different number, not a differently styled one — plus currency placement and per-currency decimals. DateTimeFormat for date order, which is genuinely ambiguous across markets, and for the time zone the value is rendered in, which should be explicit rather than the device default. RelativeTimeFormat for "3 days ago", ListFormat for joining a list with the right conjunction, and Collator for sorting, because sorting names with a default string comparison puts every accented character in the wrong place.',
      'Design for text expansion up front rather than fixing it per screen after launch. German and Finnish routinely run substantially longer than English, and short strings expand proportionally more than long ones, so buttons, tabs, table headers and anything in a fixed-width container are where it breaks. In practice that means no fixed widths on text containers, no truncation as a layout strategy where the truncated word carries the meaning, testing at a long locale rather than only at English, and accepting that some components need to wrap or stack rather than being made smaller. It is cheaper to design for it than to retrofit it into a design system already in use by several teams.',
      'Cover RTL readiness even if no RTL market is on the roadmap, because the cost of being ready is low now and high later: use CSS logical properties throughout — inline-start and inline-end rather than left and right, margin-inline rather than margin-left — so direction is a document-level attribute rather than a stylesheet fork. Flag the things logical properties do not solve, since that is the honest part: directional icons that must mirror, anything positioned with a transform, charts and progress indicators whose direction is semantic, and mixed-direction strings where a Latin product name sits inside an Arabic sentence and the bidirectional algorithm needs help. Building this way from the start costs almost nothing; adding it to an existing design system is a multi-quarter project.',
    ],
    keyPoints: [
      'Rejects concatenated sentences and makes a whole message with named placeholders the unit of translation',
      'Knows CLDR defines up to six plural categories and names languages that need more than two',
      'Routes every number, date, relative time, list and sort through the Intl APIs, including explicit time zone',
      'Designs for text expansion (German, Finnish) with no fixed-width text containers, and tests at a long locale',
      'Uses CSS logical properties for RTL readiness and names what they do not cover: mirrored icons, transforms, bidi text',
    ],
    followUps: ['A translator asks for a different word order in one language only. How does your message format handle it?', 'How would you catch a hardcoded English string before it ships?'],
